Claim 1: Women with ADHD tend to have lower self-esteem
→ Unsupported — low self-esteem is not a core or listed associated diagnostic feature of ADHD in DSM-5-TR or ICD-11; plausible as a secondary psychosocial consequence, but not established in the reference materials as a defining/sex-specific ADHD symptom
Claim 2: More likely to be daydreamers
→ Supported — matches ICD-11's inattentive presentation description, including the note that a "restrictive inattentive pattern" with daydreaming and mind-wandering can be more prominent in a subset, and ICD-11 explicitly notes females are more likely to exhibit inattentive symptoms
Claim 3: Tend to fidget in their chair a lot
→ Supported — matches ICD-11 hyperactivity criterion (difficulty sitting still, restlessness), though ICD-11 also notes males are more likely than females to show overt hyperactivity-impulsivity symptoms, so this is a somewhat inconsistent pairing with the sex-specific framing of the video
Claim 4: May deal with odd eating habits or binge eating
→ Unsupported — disordered eating patterns are not listed in DSM-5-TR or ICD-11 as an ADHD symptom or associated feature; this is not established in the reference materials as part of the ADHD clinical picture
Claim 5: More likely to be labeled as "moody" or "too sensitive"
→ Partially supported — ICD-11 does note mood lability and emotional dysregulation as associated features of ADHD, which could lead to such labeling; framed as a social perception rather than a symptom per se, but grounded in a real underlying feature
Claim 6: Difficulty with time management and organization
→ Supported — matches ICD-11 inattention criteria (difficulty planning, managing, organizing tasks and activities)
Claim 7: More sensitive to rejection and criticism
→ Unsupported — "rejection sensitivity" is a popular but non-official construct; not found in DSM-5-TR or ICD-11 as an ADHD symptom or associated feature
Claim 8: More likely to be anxious and have a "serious case of imposter syndrome"
→ Partially supported / overstated — ICD-11 and NICE do note that ADHD commonly co-occurs with anxiety disorders, so increased anxiety has a basis; however, "imposter syndrome" is not a clinical construct found in DSM-5-TR, ICD-11, or NICE, and presenting it as a defining, near-universal ADHD trait in women overstates what the evidence supports

